s (Latency vs. Context):**\n    *   **Strength:** The hybrid approach (Vector DB for semantic/long-term + Document Store for transactional/short-term) is the industry standard for robust RAG pipelines.\n    *   **Risk:** The \"Vectorization\" step creates an implicit latency penalty. If the system embeds every interaction, the cost/token ratio will spike. Relying solely on `text-embedding-3-small` without a **hybrid search** (keyword + vector) may result in \"semantic drift,\" where the agent retrieves irrelevant historical context due to noise in the vector space.\n3.  **Observability-Driven Reliability:**\n    *   The inclusion of a `logger.py` is the strongest component of this design. By implementing structured logging, you have the data necessary to calculate **Task Success Rate (TSR)** and identify where agents \"stall\" (the \"Infinite Loop\" pitfall).\n\n---\n\n### Quantifiable Metrics to Monitor\nTo move from conceptual design to operational excellence, you must track:\n*   **Agent Hand-off Latency:** Expected to be < 200ms for internal orchestration logic; > 500ms indicates inefficient state serialization.\n*   **Retrieval Hit Rate:** The percentage of queries where the retrieved context is utilized in the final output (tracked via `logger.py`).\n*   **State Bloat Index:** Measured by the size of the `AgentState` JSON per turn; any increase > 20% over 5 turns suggests a failure in summarization/truncation logic.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendations\n\n1.  **Adopt a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) for Orchestration:** Transition the `main.py` logic to a formal DAG or cyclic graph structure (e.g., *LangGraph*). This allows for parallel agent execution paths and explicit conditional branching, reducing the reliance on a single controller.\n2.  **Implement Semantic Caching:** To optimize your memory retrieval, place a **Semantic Cache (e.g., GPTCache or Redis-based)** in front of your Vector DB. This will prevent redundant LLM calls for recurring tasks, significantly re